---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    group: "Understanding Flynt",
    items: [
      {
        q: "I'm a recruiter — does Flynt help me find candidates?",
        a: "No. Flynt is not a candidate sourcing tool. Flynt is built for the business development side of recruiting — finding new client companies, not placing candidates. Specifically: Flynt identifies companies that are likely to need your recruiting services, maps the right decision-makers at those companies to approach, and surfaces signals that indicate the timing is right to reach out. Think of it this way: Flynt helps you fill your client pipeline. Once you have the client, you use your existing tools and skills to fill the role. If you are a recruiter whose primary challenge is finding and winning new clients — Flynt is built for you. If your primary challenge is sourcing candidates or managing talent pipelines, Flynt is not the right tool."
      },
      {
        q: "What exactly does Flynt do?",
        a: `Flynt is an AI-powered business development engine built specifically for recruiters and recruiting agencies. It handles the entire research and outreach layer behind finding new clients — continuously and automatically.\n\nEvery day, Flynt's AI Agent surfaces companies that posted a new job in the last 24 hours, identifies the hiring manager, enriches their contact details, and generates personalised outreach angles based on real signals — recent company news, job posting data, and company context. It then sends your outreach automatically through built-in campaign sequences, tracks every reply in a unified inbox, and manages your entire prospect pipeline through a built-in CRM.\n\nFlynt is not a candidate sourcing tool, a talent database, or a generic contact list. It is purpose-built for one job: making sure recruiters always know which companies are actively hiring, have everything they need to reach out intelligently, and never lose track of a conversation. The intelligence runs automatically. The outreach runs automatically. You focus on the relationships.`
      },
      {
        q: "How is Flynt different from Apollo, Clay, or Instantly?",
        a: `Apollo is a contact database. Instantly and Reply.io are outreach automation platforms. Clay is a flexible data workflow builder. Each of those tools solves one part of the problem — and requires you to connect the rest yourself.\n\nFlynt is the only platform built end-to-end for recruiting business development. It finds the signal — companies actively hiring right now — enriches the contact, generates a personalized outreach angle based on that specific company's news and job activity, sends the campaign, tracks the reply, and manages the pipeline. All in one place, purpose-built for recruiters.\n\nWhere Apollo gives you a list, Flynt gives you a daily ranked feed of companies in active hiring mode with the context to know exactly why and what to say. Where Clay requires you to build the workflow yourself, Flynt runs the intelligence system for you out of the box. Where Instantly sends your outreach, Flynt decides who gets it, why, and with what angle — then sends it automatically.\n\nFlynt is not a competitor to those tools. It is the intelligence and execution layer that sits where those tools assume you've already done the work. Most recruiters who use Flynt replace hours of daily manual research — not their existing stack.`
      },
      {
        q: "What is a 'hiring signal' and why does it matter for client acquisition?",
        a: "A hiring signal is a detectable event or pattern that indicates a company is about to need external recruiting help — making them a strong potential client for your recruitment business. Examples: a company raises a Series A funding round, posts 12 new engineering roles in a single month, appoints a new VP of Sales and begins scaling a commercial team, expands into a new geography, or loses their internal Head of Talent. Each of these signals suggests the company is about to have a significant hiring need — and is therefore a high-potential client acquisition target for a recruiter right now. Flynt detects these client acquisition signals before most recruiters think to look. When you reach out to a company that just triggered a relevant signal, your timing is precise and your relevance is self-evident — instead of cold. This is the intelligence edge that turns outbound into warm conversation."
      },
      {
        q: "Do I still need a CRM or outreach tool?",
        a: "Yes, in most cases. Flynt is not a CRM and does not handle outreach campaigns. Flynt is the intelligence layer. Your CRM manages the relationship. Your outreach platform sends the message. These work together — Flynt keeps structured, signal-driven opportunity flowing into the engagement stack you already use. Think of Flynt as the research engine beneath your existing tools — not a replacement for them."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    group: "Results & Expectations",
    items: [
      {
        q: "Does Flynt guarantee leads or clients?",
        a: "No. Flynt does not guarantee clients, deals, or revenue. No honest intelligence tool does. What Flynt guarantees is leverage: time reclaimed from manual research, a pipeline of active hiring signals that runs consistently, and the intelligence to know who to reach out to and why. Your results will depend on the quality of your targeting, the clarity of your outreach, and the consistency of your follow-up. Flynt prepares the work. You close the deal."
      },
      {
        q: "How quickly can I expect to feel the impact?",
        a: `Immediately. Set up your agent in under 5 minutes and your first leads arrive the same day — active hiring managers at companies that posted jobs in the last 24 hours, enriched and ready to reach out to. The manual research layer is gone from day one.\n\nGetting responses and converting leads into client relationships is a different timeline. Like any business development motion, consistent daily and weekly outreach over two to three months is typically when recruiters start seeing meaningful activity — replies, conversations, and new opportunities coming through. Some see it sooner, some later — it depends on your niche, your outreach quality, and how consistently you work the leads Flynt delivers.\n\nFlynt removes the groundwork so your effort goes further. The results depend on showing up every day.`
      },
      {
        q: "What if my outreach isn't working right now — will Flynt fix that?",
        a: "Not on its own. If your targeting is off, your offer is unclear, or your messaging isn't resonating, Flynt will surface better-qualified targets — but it will not repair the execution layer. What it will do is make testing and iteration faster. Better intelligence means faster feedback on what works and what doesn't. Flynt amplifies good execution. It doesn't substitute for it."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    group: "Is Flynt Right For Me?",
    items: [
      {
        q: "Who gets the most out of Flynt?",
        a: "Operators who understand that revenue comes from consistent, disciplined execution — and who want infrastructure that supports that motion. Specifically: recruiters and recruitment agencies running active outbound, sales and GTM teams responsible for account-level prospecting, agency owners building repeatable client acquisition systems, and revenue leaders who want to remove the research burden from their team's day. Flynt creates the most value when it is paired with consistent outreach, clear targeting, and a genuine commitment to follow-up."
      },
      {
        q: "I've never done business development before — can Flynt help me?",
        a: "Flynt can reduce the friction and overwhelm of getting started. It gives you a structured system so the operational burden doesn't become a barrier to action. What it does not do is teach you how to sell, position your offer, or handle objections. You will still need to develop those skills. For experienced operators, Flynt increases speed and consistency. For those building their first BD motion, it removes the research complexity so you can focus on learning the craft of selling — not figuring out who to contact each morning."
      },
      {
        q: "Can Flynt work for teams outside of recruiting?",
        a: "Yes — as long as the use case is client acquisition. Any operator whose growth depends on identifying and winning new clients can use Flynt effectively. Sales teams use Flynt to surface signal-ready prospect accounts before outreach begins. GTM operators use it to identify expansion and partnership opportunities. B2B service agencies use it to maintain a consistent client pipeline without manual research cycles. Flynt was built first for the recruiting vertical — specifically for the business development challenge of finding new client companies, not candidate sourcing. That same client acquisition intelligence infrastructure applies to any operator running a disciplined outbound or account-based acquisition motion. The common thread across every use case: Flynt is always used to find new clients, never to find candidates, talent, or job seekers."
      },
      {
        q: "What makes the difference between users who succeed with Flynt and those who don't?",
        a: "Three fundamentals: a clearly defined target market, consistent outreach and follow-up, and willingness to refine messaging over time. Flynt handles the intelligence preparation. The operators who succeed are the ones who show up to act on it — consistently, with discipline, and with a genuine commitment to improving their execution. Flynt provides leverage. Operators provide the will to use it."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    group: "Practical Questions",
    items: [
      {
        q: "What tools does Flynt replace?",
        a: "Flynt replaces the fragmented manual research layer that most revenue teams currently stitch together using a mix of spreadsheets, scraping tools, enrichment platforms, and tab-heavy research sessions. It is not a CRM. It is not an email sender. It is the continuously operating intelligence system that prepares your opportunity universe — replacing the hours of repetitive groundwork that precede every good outreach conversation."
      },
      {
        q: "What happens if I cancel my subscription?",
        a: "Your subscription ends at the close of your billing period and your access stops. There is no lock-in beyond your subscription term. That said, most teams find that once their opportunity flow is structured and their research burden is removed, returning to manual processes feels like a significant step backward. The real cost of leaving Flynt is not the subscription price — it is the hours of research time that return to your plate."
      },
      {
        q: "Is Flynt worth it if I only prospect occasionally?",
        a: "Probably not — at least not yet. Flynt creates the most value for operators who are actively and consistently generating revenue. If you are only reaching out to prospects occasionally, the full leverage of a continuously running intelligence system may be more than your current motion requires. When you are ready to make prospecting a consistent, systemized part of your business — Flynt will be ready for you."
      }
    ]
  }
];
---

<Layout
  title="FAQ — Flynt AI"
  description="Every question about Flynt answered clearly. Understand what Flynt is, who it's for, and what results to expect."
>

<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="tag">FAQ</div>
    <h1>Every question. Answered <span class="accent">clearly.</span></h1>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section faq-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="faq-layout">

      <!-- Sticky group nav -->
      <aside class="faq-nav">
        {faqs.map(group => (
          <a href={`#${group.group.replace(/\s+/g, '-').toLowerCase()}`} class="faq-nav__link">
            {group.group}
          </a>
        ))}
      </aside>

      <!-- FAQ Groups -->
      <div class="faq-groups">
        {faqs.map(group => (
          <div class="faq-group" id={group.group.replace(/\s+/g, '-').toLowerCase()}>
            <h3 class="faq-group__title">{group.group}</h3>
            <div class="faq-items">
              {group.items.map(item => (
                <details class="faq-item">
                  <summary class="faq-item__q">{item.q}</summary>
                  <div class="faq-item__a">
                    {item.a.split('\n\n').map(para => <p>{para}</p>)}
                  </div>
                </details>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- CTA at bottom -->
    <div class="faq-cta">
      <p>Still have questions?</p>
      <a href="mailto:info@tryflynt.ai" class="btn btn--outline">Contact Us</a>
      <a href="https://app.tryflynt.ai/" class="btn btn--primary">Get Started</a>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

<style>
.page-hero {
  padding: 80px 24px 64px;
}

.page-hero h1 {
  margin: 12px 0 0;
  max-width: 600px;
  font-size: 3rem;
}

.accent {
  color: var(--purple);
  -webkit-text-stroke: 0;
}

.faq-section {
  padding-top: 0;
}

.faq-layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr;
  gap: 64px;
  align-items: start;
}

/* Sticky nav */
.faq-nav {
  position: sticky;
  top: 88px;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 4px;
}

.faq-nav__link {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-size: 1rem;
  font-weight: 500;
  color: var(--gray-500);
  padding: 8px 12px;
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  transition: all 0.15s;
  text-decoration: none;
  border-left: 2px solid transparent;
}

.faq-nav__link:hover {
  color: var(--purple);
  background: var(--purple-bg);
  border-left-color: var(--purple);
}

/* FAQ Groups */
.faq-groups {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 56px;
}

.faq-group__title {
  color: var(--black);
  margin-bottom: 20px;
  padding-bottom: 12px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--slate-200);
}

.faq-items {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 2px;
}

/* Accordion */
.faq-item {
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  overflow: hidden;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  transition: border-color 0.2s;
}

.faq-item[open] {
  border-color: var(--gray-200);
  background: var(--white);
}

.faq-item__q {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-size: 1rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--gray-800);
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: 18px 20px;
  list-style: none;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 16px;
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  transition: background 0.15s;
}

.faq-item__q::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }

.faq-item__q::after {
  content: '+';
  font-size: 1.3rem;
  font-weight: 400;
  color: var(--gray-400);
  flex-shrink: 0;
  transition: transform 0.2s;
}

.faq-item[open] .faq-item__q::after {
  transform: rotate(45deg);
  color: var(--purple);
}

.faq-item__q:hover {
  background: var(--gray-50);
}

.faq-item__a {
  padding: 0 20px 20px;
}

.faq-item__a p {
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  line-height: 1.7;
  color: var(--gray-600);
  margin: 0 0 12px;
}

.faq-item__a p:last-child {
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

/* CTA */
.faq-cta {
  margin-top: 72px;
  padding: 40px;
  background: var(--purple-bg);
  border-radius: var(--radius-xl);
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 16px;
  border: 1px solid var(--purple-bg-2);
}

.faq-cta p {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-size: 1.1rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--gray-800);
  margin-right: auto;
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .faq-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .faq-nav { display: none; }
  .faq-cta { flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; }
  .faq-cta p { margin-right: 0; }
}
</style>
